    "body": "Savannah/Hilton Head International Airport opened a 36-acre air cargo complex last week, adding two freight buildings and space for more cargo carriers. The Savannah Area Chamber of Commerce put the cost at about $80 million, including a taxiway extension and a new bridge over Gulfstream Road.\nWhat’s Related\nThe complex sits at the north end of the airport and has direct access to the runway and the apron, where planes park to load and unload. It can accommodate wide-body aircraft such as the Boeing 767-300 and Airbus A300-600. The site also has cold storage infrastructure, and the airport is marketing space to freight forwarders, cargo airlines and companies that handle temperature-sensitive shipments .\nFedEx and UPS signed on early\nThe larger cargo building spans about 65,000 square feet and was designed for a single tenant, with space for sorting packages, offices, and customer service. The airport said in its 2024 annual report that FedEx had committed to the single-tenant building and UPS had signed for 15,000 square feet in the other building, which was designed for multiple carriers.\nAt the opening, the airport listed three units available for lease, totaling 45,000 square feet. The largest is 25,000 square feet, and the other two are 10,000 square feet each. The units have truck docks on one side and access to the aircraft apron on the other, giving tenants a direct route for moving freight between trucks and planes.\nPlanes can enter and leave at the same time\nThe airport extended Taxiway G and built a bridge over Gulfstream Road so one aircraft can enter the cargo area while another leaves. The work added about 13,500 square yards of taxiway pavement, along with lighting, signs, pavement markings, and drainage.\nThe project took about five years from the first discussions through construction, Savannah Airport Commission Executive Director Greg Kelly told WJCL. Kelly told WTOC that grants covered about $10 million of the project and that the airport paid for the buildings out of its own budget.\nClose to port and two highways\nThe airport sits along Interstate 95 and less than 10 miles from Interstate 16, which gives trucks a route west toward Atlanta. The cargo complex is also minutes from the Port of Savannah and two major rail lines, putting it near the region’s other freight connections.\nThe airport handled 1,236 tons of cargo in October, up about 12% from the same month a year earlier. Through the first 10 months of 2026, however, it handled about 10,400 tons, roughly even with the same period in 2025.",
